import {lesson as L, sources as S} from './format.mjs';
export const systems = [
L('os-1','Processes and scheduling','schedule',S.os,
'Distinguish running, ready and blocked, and compare process completion under two scheduling policies.',
'A CPU can switch between programs quickly enough to make their work appear simultaneous. The operating system decides who gets time on each core and keeps enough state to resume work later.',
['A process owns an address space and resources. A thread is an execution stream with its own registers, stack and program counter. Threads in one process generally share code and heap memory.',
 'A ready thread can run when scheduled. A running thread can block while waiting for input. A completed operation can make a blocked thread ready again. A timer interrupt gives the kernel a chance to preempt running code.',
 'A context switch saves execution state and restores the next thread’s state. Round robin lets each ready thread run for a time slice before giving another a turn. First-come first-served runs each eligible job to completion in the simple lab model.',
 'Response time measures how soon a process first gets CPU. Turnaround measures completion time relative to arrival. A low time quantum can improve responsiveness but adds switching overhead.'],
'Jobs A=5, B=2 and C=1 all arrive at t=0. With round robin and quantum 2 the CPU sequence is A A B B C A A A. C finishes at t=5. With first-come first-served, C finishes at t=8.',
'A blocked thread cannot make progress even if a CPU is idle. It needs the event it is waiting on before it can be scheduled.',
'The scheduler picks ready threads. Preemption enables sharing. Compare policies with response and turnaround.',
['Which metric measures the wait until a process first runs?',['Turnaround','Response time','CPU burst length'],1,'Response time is first-run time minus arrival. Turnaround uses completion instead of first run.','Find the time of the initial response.']),
L('os-2','Virtual memory and page tables','pages',S.os,
'Translate a virtual address and identify an unmapped virtual page.',
'A process has its own map of memory. Two processes can use the same numeric address while the CPU accesses different physical locations for each.',
['Split the virtual address into a virtual page number and an offset. The page table maps a page to a physical frame and records permissions. The offset is preserved by translation.',
 'The translation lookaside buffer caches recent translations. A TLB miss normally requires another lookup or a software handler, depending on the architecture. A page fault occurs when translation finds an absent mapping or a disallowed access.',
 'With a 4096-byte page, the low 12 address bits are the offset. A mapping from page 2 to frame 7 translates address 0x2345 to 0x7345. Real systems can use several page sizes and multilevel tables.',
 'Fault processing can allocate a page lazily, bring data into RAM, or reject an invalid access. This is part of both performance and process isolation.'],
'Use the lab to translate a mapped address. Then try an unmapped page and inspect what the lab reports. Change the page size while keeping the same numeric address, and explain which fields change.',
'A TLB miss and a page fault mean different things. A valid mapping might already exist in the page table even when the TLB lacks it.',
'Find page and offset, check the mapping and permissions, then combine frame and offset.',
['What does a valid address translation preserve?',['Virtual page number','Page offset','Physical frame number from a previous process'],1,'The mapping replaces the page number with a frame number while keeping the position inside the page.','Which bits pick a byte within the page?']),
L('languages-1','C, Rust and ownership','lifetime',S.rust,
'Compare manual lifetime management with Rust ownership and explain why a borrow can become invalid.',
'An object often outlives the expression that produced it. Someone has to decide who owns it, who can access it and when its storage can be reused. C and Rust answer those questions with different enforcement points.',
['In C, automatic variables usually have lifetimes tied to scope, while dynamically allocated storage stays live until freed. Pointers carry addresses, but the type system generally does not track which aliases survive a free.',
 'In Rust, a non-Copy value can move to a new binding, transferring ownership. A shared borrow allows reading. An exclusive mutable borrow permits mutation while excluding conflicting access. The compiler checks many such relations statically.',
 'When an owning String goes out of scope, its destructor releases its heap buffer. String data can be borrowed as &str without making a new independent buffer. A borrow cannot outlive the data it refers to.',
 'Rust’s safety guarantees can depend on unsafe implementations and foreign code honoring their contracts. It also permits memory leaks. A leak retains storage, whereas a dangling pointer refers to storage whose lifetime ended.'],
'Compare letting owner a drop while b still refers to the same object with moving ownership from a to b. In a move, dropping a does not release the resource because a no longer owns it. This logic matters in long-running servers and concurrent code.',
'Copying a pointer usually does not clone the referenced bytes or extend their lifetime.',
'Ownership controls cleanup. Borrows make temporary access explicit. Aliases need a valid lifetime.',
['What changes after moving a Rust String from a to b?',['Both names own the same allocation','b owns the value and a cannot be used as its previous owner','The heap bytes necessarily get copied'],1,'A move transfers ownership of the value, including responsibility for its heap allocation.','Distinguish copying a resource from transferring ownership.']),
L('compilers-1','Tokens, parsing and syntax trees','parser',S.llvm,
'Explain how a lexer and parser represent precedence in a syntax tree.',
'A compiler needs to recognize meanings like “multiply before adding” even though the text is just characters. It first groups characters into tokens, then applies a grammar to those tokens.',
['A lexer emits units such as number, identifier, plus and left parenthesis. Lexing can reject characters that the language does not recognize.',
 'A parser recognizes valid combinations and forms a tree. An expression grammar can use terms for multiplication and expressions for lower-priority addition. Parentheses ask the parser to treat their contents as one expression.',
 'In 2 + 3 × 4, addition belongs at the root, with multiplication as its right subtree. Evaluating the right subtree first gives 12 and then addition produces 14.',
 'An AST omits surface details that are unnecessary for later passes. A type checker, interpreter or IR generator can walk the same shape and interpret it differently.'],
'In the lab, compare 2+3*4 with (2+3)*4. The lexer still emits the same numbers and operators in both cases, but parentheses lead the parser to construct a different AST. Results differ: 14 and 20.',
'Token order alone does not specify precedence. The grammar and parse algorithm define grouping.',
'Lex characters into tokens, parse tokens into a tree, then analyze or execute tree structure.',
['What is the root operator in the AST for (1+2)*3?',['+','*','The left parenthesis'],1,'Parentheses group 1+2 into a subtree, leaving multiplication as the top operation.','Which operation combines the grouped result and 3?']),
L('algorithms-1','Complexity, search and sorting','search',S.alg,
'Explain the sorted-input invariant for binary search and estimate comparison counts.',
'Searching a shuffled list can make you examine every item. In an ordered list, a single comparison can rule out about half of the remaining items.',
['Binary search maintains an interval that must still contain any match. Compare the key with the midpoint. A smaller midpoint eliminates that item and everything to its left. A larger midpoint eliminates it and everything to its right.',
 'Use lo + floor((hi − lo)/2) for a midpoint in fixed-width arithmetic to avoid adding two large bounds. Decide whether bounds are inclusive before writing the update rules.',
 'Each iteration cuts down the remaining interval. The worst comparison count grows logarithmically. Linear search can inspect n entries. A presort or a maintained index makes sense when enough queries reuse that ordering.',
 'Sorting also has costs. Merge sort uses n log n comparisons in the worst case but usually needs extra storage for arrays. Insertion sort can be efficient for small or nearly sorted arrays.'],
'Search for 12 in [1, 4, 8, 12, 20, 27]. The midpoint at index 2 is 8, so keep indices 3–5. The next midpoint is 20, so keep index 3. Use the lab to inspect the interval each step.',
'Binary search does not make arbitrary data searchable in logarithmic time. It requires sorted order and efficient access to the middle.',
'Write down the invariant. Use the comparison to discard half. Account for the cost of preparing the input.',
['Which item can be discarded when a sorted ascending array’s midpoint is less than the target?',['Only the midpoint','The midpoint and everything left of it','The midpoint and everything right of it'],1,'All items on the left are at most the midpoint, so none can equal the larger target.','What does ascending order tell you about the left side?']),
L('networking-1','Packets, Ethernet and routing','route',S.ip,
'Follow one IPv4 packet across links and explain how a router uses destination prefixes and TTL.',
'A packet can cross several physical networks on the way to its destination. Each local link needs instructions for delivering the next piece of the journey. The IP destination describes the final endpoint.',
['Ethernet delivers a frame on a local link using link-layer addresses. A sender determines the next hop, resolves its local link address when needed, and encapsulates the IP packet inside a frame.',
 'A router removes the incoming link-layer envelope, examines the destination IP address and looks up a route. If multiple prefixes match, it normally chooses the longest prefix, subject to the routing table’s policy.',
 'At each IPv4 forwarding hop the router decrements TTL and updates the header checksum. If TTL expires, the packet is discarded. A new link-layer envelope carries the packet on the outgoing link.',
 'In the simple path in the lab, IP source and destination stay fixed. A translator can change addresses in operational networks. TTL bounds the time a packet can be forwarded through routing loops.'],
'For destination 10.2.1.9, both 10.0.0.0/8 and 10.2.1.0/24 match. The /24 is more specific. Step the lab with TTL 1, then 3, and compare whether the packet survives two routers.',
'A MAC address is used on a local link. It does not generally stay attached to the packet across routed networks.',
'Frames serve local delivery. Routers forward IP packets by destination prefix. TTL bounds forwarding.',
['Which route is more specific for 10.2.1.9?',['10.0.0.0/8','10.2.1.0/24','0.0.0.0/0'],1,'A /24 fixes 24 leading bits, more than the /8 or default route.','Count the fixed prefix bits.']),
L('databases-1','B-trees and indexing','index',S.sql,
'Follow a point lookup through an index and explain the tradeoffs of maintaining it.',
'An index is extra structure that can find matching rows without reading the entire table. An ordered tree groups comparisons into pages so each read can rule out much of the key space.',
['A typical B-tree page contains multiple sorted keys. An internal page has separators and child references. To find a key, pick the appropriate child interval. High fanout keeps the number of levels small.',
 'In a B+-tree style model, data entries live in leaves. A point lookup reaches one leaf and checks its keys. A range scan starts at the relevant leaf and continues through neighboring entries.',
 'An index entry can point to a row location. A query may still need to fetch the base row. A covering index can supply fields directly when visibility and storage rules allow it.',
 'Indexes have write and storage costs. Inserts may split pages, add log records and affect cache pressure. A table scan can be faster if a query needs most of the rows.'],
'Find 19 in the lab’s root with split keys 10 and 20. Choose the middle child, then compare the sorted leaf entries. Consider how the path differs for a key missing between 10 and 20.',
'A declared index gives the planner a way to access rows. The planner may choose another plan based on selectivity and cost.',
'Use high fanout to reduce page reads. Compare point lookups, range scans and update costs.',
['Why might a planner scan the table when a matching index exists?',['The requested set includes most rows','An index always ignores numeric values','Trees cannot support equality'],0,'Many index lookups can mean many base-row page accesses. A sequential scan can be cheaper.','Consider the I/O needed after the index returns matches.']),
L('distributed-1','Threads, atomics and async','race',S.threads,
'Trace a lost update and explain the different jobs of atomics and async executors.',
'Two workers can each follow reasonable instructions and still produce a wrong result together. Correctness can depend on which actions other workers can observe between two lines of code.',
['An increment usually requires reading a value, adding one and writing it back. If two workers read zero before either writes, both can write one and one update is lost.',
 'A mutex can make a critical section exclusive. Atomic read-modify-write operations such as fetch-add give the increment a single indivisible effect with respect to operations on that atomic location.',
 'Atomicity of one location does not automatically preserve invariants covering multiple fields. Memory ordering determines which related operations become visible across threads and when.',
 'An async task can yield while it waits for I/O. An executor resumes tasks when their progress becomes possible. Async can multiplex work on one thread or many threads, and it does not by itself make shared data safe.'],
'Step A reads, B reads, A writes, B writes in the lab. Then switch to atomic increment and compare outcomes. Use this mental model to review request counters and queues.',
'An async function can still do long CPU-bound work. If it does not yield or offload the work, it can delay other tasks on the same executor thread.',
'Separate concurrency from parallelism. Protect shared invariants. Use a read-modify-write when a single counter needs atomic updates.',
['Why can two ordinary increments starting from zero finish at one?',['Both workers can read the same old value','Addition is non-deterministic','The scheduler disables arithmetic'],0,'Both reads may see zero. Each computes and writes one, overwriting the other result.','Expand one increment into read, compute and write.']),
L('security-1','Protection and memory safety','bounds',S.rust,
'Apply bounds and lifetime rules to program data and identify where privilege checks belong.',
'A program needs access to the bytes it owns and the operations its user permits. Security depends on specifying those limits and enforcing them at the point where the operation occurs.',
['Memory safety includes bounds safety and lifetime safety. An array access must be within the allocated range. A reference must point to valid live storage for the duration of access.',
 'Typed safe interfaces encode invariants. For example, Rust slices track length alongside a reference, and get returns None when a requested element is out of range. Native interfaces need explicit ownership and length contracts.',
 'Hardware protection distinguishes execution modes and page permissions. A user application asks the kernel to perform privileged operations using controlled entry points. The kernel validates arguments and checks the requested operation against permissions.',
 'Authentication establishes identity or verifies a credential. Authorization decides what that identity can do. Least privilege limits the operations granted to each component. Defense in depth maintains checks across layers.'],
'A service that returns bytes from a user-supplied offset can validate offset and requested length against the buffer before slicing. One robust comparison checks length ≤ size − offset after establishing offset ≤ size, avoiding overflow in offset + length.',
'Authenticating a caller does not by itself establish access to a particular record. Check authorization for the resource and operation.',
'Check bounds and lifetimes for memory. Check identity and authority where actions cross a trust boundary.',
['Which slice [offset, offset+length) fits in a 16-byte buffer?',['Offset 14 and length 4','Offset 14 and length 2','Offset 17 and length 0'],1,'It covers byte indices 14 and 15. Its exclusive end is 16, which is permitted.','The upper endpoint of a half-open interval is excluded.'])
];
